    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 60)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across five decades of British BBC Saturday Night Theatre drama and American modern audio drama history (1957–1997):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 60)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1957.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Murder When Necessary</code>, <code>Philip Levene BBC</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Murder When Necessary (Philip Levene — Patrick Barr — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Home</strong><br>1957-08-24<br>9:15 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Drama Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Patrick Barr</strong>, <strong>Beryl Baxter</strong>, <strong>Hamilton Dyce</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Philip Levene</strong> (Writer), <strong>Audrey Cameron</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A tense psychological thriller written by Philip Levene, wherein an apparently flawless murder scheme conceived by a desperate blackmailer begins to unravel when an uninvited guest turns up at a secluded Surrey country estate on a stormy evening.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1963.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Means to an End</code>, <code>John Rowan Wilson BBC</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Means to an End (John Rowan Wilson — John Carson — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Home</strong><br>1963-08-24<br>8:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Drama Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>John Carson</strong>, <strong>Mary Wimbush</strong>, <strong>Ralph Truman</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Rowan Wilson</strong>, <strong>John Wilson</strong>, <strong>Norman Wright</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Corporate&quot;, &quot;Ethics&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>An intense corporate thriller chronicling an ambitious young executive who uncovers systemic bribery and international bribery kickbacks within a transatlantic industrial manufacturing conglomerate, forcing him to choose between corporate advancement and personal integrity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1968.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>French Prisoner</code>, <code>Martin Jarvis BBC 1968</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The French Prisoner (Martin Jarvis &amp; Sean Barrett — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1968-08-24<br>8:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Drama Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Martin Jarvis</strong>, <strong>Sean Barrett</strong>, <strong>Ronald Herdman</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Elliot</strong> (Writer), <strong>David H. Godfrey</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;, &quot;Napoleonic&quot;, &quot;British&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Set during the Napoleonic Wars on the windswept coast of Cornwall, a compassionate English physician shelters an escaped French naval prisoner of war, sparking fierce suspicion among local smugglers and coastal militia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1974.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Soldier Poor Man</code>, <code>Ted Willis BBC 1974</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Soldier, Poor Man, Beggar Man, Thief (Ted Willis — Bryan Pringle — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1974-08-24<br>8:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Drama Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Bryan Pringle</strong>, <strong>Peter Vaughan</strong>, <strong>Liz Goulding</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lord Ted Willis</strong> (Writer), <strong>Joe Burroughs</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Social&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Post-War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A gritty post-war social drama by Ted Willis following a demobilized British army soldier struggling to find honest employment in London&#39;s East End, who finds himself drawn into an underworld safe-cracking gang by former wartime comrades.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1985.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Rookery Nook</code>, <code>Ian Carmichael Derek Nimmo</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Rookery Nook (Ben Travers — Ian Carmichael &amp; Derek Nimmo — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1985-08-24<br>8:00 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Comedy Farce</td>\n<td><strong>Ian Carmichael</strong>, <strong>Derek Nimmo</strong>, <strong>Joan Sanderson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ben Travers</strong> (Author), <strong>Peter Jones</strong>, <strong>Gerry Jones</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Farce&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Theater&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Ben Travers&#39; legendary Aldwych farce starring comedy masters Ian Carmichael and Derek Nimmo, following a newly married groom whose holiday cottage is invaded by a distressed young woman in pink silk pyjamas, sparking frantic cover-ups to deceive a ferocious daily housekeeper and suspicious in-laws.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Imagination_Theater_1997.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Crisis Star Light</code>, <code>Jim French Crisis Series</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Crisis: Comedy in Three Acts &amp; Star Light, Star Bright (Jim French — Imagination Theater)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1997-08-24<br>8:00 PM PDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Imagination Theater</td>\n<td><strong>John Gilbert</strong>, <strong>Larry Albert</strong>, <strong>Pat French</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jim French</strong> (Writer/Dir.), <strong>Eric Aho</strong> (Composer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Audio drama master Jim French presents two gripping suspense thrillers in the <em>Crisis</em> anthology series: a deadly stage farce in &#39;Comedy in Three Acts&#39; and a tense psychological kidnapping mystery in &#39;Star Light, Star Bright&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 360</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
