    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 52)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, cinematic parody, vocal jazz, and Latin American historical drama radio history (1934–1958):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 52)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Guiding_Light_1950_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bill Refuses Teds Loan</code>, <code>Guiding Light Duz 1950</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Bill Refuses Ted&#39;s Loan (Lyle Sudrow as Bill Bauer — Duz)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1950-08-24<br>1:45 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Duz Soap — &quot;Duz Does It&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Lyle Sudrow</strong> (Bill Bauer), <strong>Charita Bauer</strong>, <strong>James Lipton</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Irna Phillips</strong> (Creator), <strong>Agnes Nixon</strong>, <strong>Bud Collyer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Soap Opera&quot;, &quot;Serial&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Guiding Light, created by Irna Phillips...&quot; In the small town of Five Points, tensions flare in the Bauer household when Bill Bauer flatly refuses Ted White&#39;s desperate request for a personal loan, suspecting illicit gambling debts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lands_Free_1942_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Francisco de Miranda</code>, <code>Lands of the Free 1942</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Francisco de Miranda: Liberty, Equality, Fraternity (NBC Inter-American University)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1942-08-24<br>10:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Inter-American Univ.</td>\n<td><strong>Luis Van Rooten</strong> (Miranda), <strong>Santos Ortega</strong>, <strong>Juano Hernandez</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Morton Wishengrad</strong> (Writer), <strong>Homer Fickett</strong>, <strong>Gene Hamilton</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Biography&quot;, &quot;Latin America&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>An educational historical dramatization recounting the revolutionary life of Venezuelan patriot Francisco de Miranda, exploring his military campaigns across the French Revolution and his relentless fight for South American independence from Spanish colonial rule.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Jack_Benny_1934_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>House Of Rawchild</code>, <code>Jack Benny Chevrolet 1934</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The House of Rawchild (Jack Benny &amp; Mary Livingstone — Chevrolet)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1934-08-24<br>10:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Chevrolet Motor Co.</strong><br>1934 Chevrolet Six Cars</td>\n<td><strong>Jack Benny</strong>, <strong>Mary Livingstone</strong>, <strong>Frank Parker</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Harry Conn</strong> (Writer), <strong>Alois Havrilla</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Vaudeville&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Jack Benny and Mary Livingstone present a hysterical cinematic parody of <em>The House of Rothschild</em> titled &#39;The House of Rawchild,&#39; featuring Jack as an inept European financier trying to secure loans from tenors and band musicians for Chevrolet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Crosby_1955_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode 198</code>, <code>Bing Crosby Buddy Cole 1955</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Song Is You (Bing Crosby with the Buddy Cole Trio — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1955-08-24<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>General Electric</strong><br>&amp; CBS Sustaining</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole and his Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Producer/Dir.), <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Der Bingle eases into an intimate summer session with the Buddy Cole Trio, crooning relaxed renditions of &#39;The Song Is You&#39; and swapping laid-back Hollywood anecdotes with announcer Ken Carpenter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Crosby_1956_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bing and Rosie Ford</code>, <code>Bing Crosby Show 1956</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Summer Duets with Rosemary Clooney (Bing Crosby — Ford)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1956-08-24<br>7:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Ford Motor Company</strong><br>Fairlane &amp; Thunderbird</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Rosemary Clooney</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Producer), <strong>Murdo MacKenzie</strong>, <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Duet&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Bing Crosby teams up with guest songstress Rosemary Clooney and the Buddy Cole Trio for charming vocal duets, solo jazz interpretations, and humorous radio banter for the Ford Motor Company.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Crosby_1958_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Shadow of Ashland E05</code>, <code>Bing Crosby Americana</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Pacific Northwest Americana (Bing Crosby &amp; Buddy Cole Trio — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1958-08-24<br>7:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>CBS Summer Showcase</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Producer), <strong>Murdo MacKenzie</strong>, <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Bing Crosby performs nostalgic American folk standards and Tin Pan Alley gems accompanied by organist and pianist Buddy Cole, introducing musical vignettes celebrating Pacific Northwest Americana.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 312</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
