    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 43)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, espionage thriller, comedy quiz, and British sitcom radio history (1935–1969):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 43)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Lone_Ranger_1955_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Masked Partners</code>, <code>Brace Beemer Cheerios 1955</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Masked Partners (Starring Brace Beemer as The Lone Ranger — Cheerios)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WABC)</strong><br>1955-08-24<br>7:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>General Mills, Inc.</strong><br>Cheerios &amp; Sugar Jets</td>\n<td><strong>Brace Beemer</strong> (Lone Ranger), <strong>John Todd</strong> (Tonto)</td>\n<td><strong>Fran Striker</strong> (Writer), <strong>Charles D. Livingstone</strong>, <strong>Fred Foy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Hero&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;A fiery horse with the speed of light, a cloud of dust, and a hearty Hi-Yo, Silver!&quot; The Lone Ranger and Tonto ride into a range conflict where an outlaw gang uses black masks to masquerade as the legendary vigilantes and rob local mining claims.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FDR_1935_08_24_B.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Young Democrat Speech</code>, <code>FDR Young Democrats 1935</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;President Franklin D. Roosevelt: Address to Young Democrats (1935)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS &amp; NBC</strong><br>1935-08-24<br>10:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Presidential Address</td>\n<td><strong>President Franklin D. Roosevelt</strong> (Speaker)</td>\n<td><strong>White House Diplomatic Reception Room Broadcast</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Speech&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Political&quot;, &quot;Public Affairs&quot;, &quot;Presidential&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>President Franklin D. Roosevelt addresses the National Convention of Young Democratic Clubs in Milwaukee from the White House, urging young Americans to reject reactionary obstruction and champion the New Deal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stop_or_Go_1944_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Stop or Go 1944</code>, <code>Joe E Brown Traffic Quiz</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Traffic Signal Quiz (Starring Joe E. Brown — Bexel Vitamins)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Net (WJZ)</strong><br>1944-08-24<br>8:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>McKesson &amp; Robbins</strong><br>Bexel B-Complex</td>\n<td><strong>Joe E. Brown</strong> (Master of Ceremonies)</td>\n<td><strong>Ruthrauff &amp; Ryan</strong>, <strong>Dave Elton</strong>, <strong>Ken Niles</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Quiz&quot;, &quot;Game Show&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Audience Participation&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Wide-mouthed comedy star Joe E. Brown hosts the hilarious audience-participation traffic-signal quiz game <em>Stop or Go</em>, challenging contestant drivers and pedestrians to answer rapid-fire general knowledge questions before the traffic signal turns red for Bexel Vitamins.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Counterspy_1942_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>No Broadcast</code>, <code>Shipyard Saboteurs Counterspy</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Munitions Shipyard Saboteurs (Don MacLaughlin as Counterspy — Blue Net)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Net (WJZ)</strong><br>1942-08-24<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Blue Network Feature</td>\n<td><strong>Don MacLaughlin</strong> (David Harding), <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Creator), <strong>Leonard Bass</strong>, <strong>Bob Shepherd</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Espionage&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;World War II&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Washington, calling David Harding, Counterspy!&quot; Chief David Harding and agent Harry Peters smash a covert Axis sabotage ring attempting to detonate underwater depth charges in a critical East Coast munitions shipyard.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Counterspy_1947_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Counterspy 1947</code>, <code>Microfilm Ring Bit-O-Honey</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Jet Propulsion Microfilm Ring (Don MacLaughlin as Counterspy — Bit-O-Honey)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-24<br>5:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Schutter Candy Co.</strong><br>Bit-O-Honey Candy</td>\n<td><strong>Don MacLaughlin</strong> (David Harding), <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Creator), <strong>Leonard Bass</strong>, <strong>Bob Shepherd</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Espionage&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Cold War&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>United States Counterspy chief David Harding uncovers a post-war smuggling network trafficking classified aeronautical jet propulsion microfilms through a ring of dummy import-export houses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Clitheroe_1969_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Take A Running Walk</code>, <code>Jimmy Clitheroe BBC 1969</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Take a Running Walk (Starring Jimmy Clitheroe — BBC Radio 2)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 2</strong><br>1969-08-24<br>2:00 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Light Entertainment</td>\n<td><strong>Jimmy Clitheroe</strong>, <strong>Peter Sinclair</strong>, <strong>Patricia Burke</strong></td>\n<td><strong>James Casey &amp; Frank Roscoe</strong>, <strong>James Casey</strong>, <strong>John Dunn</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Slapstick&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Pint-sized cheeky schoolboy Jimmy Clitheroe stirs up chaotic domestic mayhem in Lancashire when he tricks silly brother-in-law Alfie into entering an absurd walking race across the moors to win a fake trophy.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 258</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
