    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 17)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, British BBC Radio 4/Light Programme, and educational broadcasting history (1940–1998):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 17)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Man_Soup_1998_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Slomzovakia Aubergine</code>, <code>Mark Tavener Soup</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Welcome to Slomzovakia, Home of the Aubergine (Mark Tavener — Man of Soup)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1998-08-24<br>11:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Radio 4 Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Robert Bathurst</strong> (Frank Bradley), <strong>Simon Greenall</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mark Tavener</strong> (Writer), <strong>Paul Schlesinger</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Culinary&quot;, &quot;Political&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Frank Bradley travels to the impoverished Eastern European republic of Slomzovakia to investigate their national aubergine soup industry, finding himself caught in a bizarre political coup orchestrated by rival vegetable oligarchs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TIFH_1948_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Balling The Jack</code>, <code>Jimmy Edwards Dick Bentley</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Take It from Here: Ballin&#39; the Jack (Jimmy Edwards &amp; Dick Bentley)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Light Prog.</strong><br>1948-08-24<br>8:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Light Programme</td>\n<td><strong>Jimmy Edwards</strong>, <strong>Dick Bentley</strong>, <strong>Joy Nichols</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frank Muir &amp; Denis Norden</strong>, <strong>Ronald Fletcher</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Sketch&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Jimmy Edwards, Dick Bentley, and Joy Nichols trade rapid-fire British radio comedy sketches, satirical parodies of London dance hall crazes including &#39;Ballin&#39; the Jack&#39;, and hilarious musical interruptions with Billy Ternent&#39;s Orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Official_Det_1948_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Murder at Roadhouse</code>, <code>Craig McDonnell Britton</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Murder at the Roadhouse (Starring Craig McDonnell as Dan Britton)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1948-08-24<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Official Detective Stories</td>\n<td><strong>Craig McDonnell</strong> (Dan Britton), <strong>Tom Tully</strong></td>\n<td><strong>William Sweets</strong> (Writer), <strong>Frank Gallop</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;True Crime&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Detective Lieutenant Dan Britton investigates the fatal shooting of a night watchman at an isolated highway roadhouse, using tire-tread casts and ballistics comparisons to shatter the alibi of a ruthless interstate robbery crew.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sam_Spade_1947_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Jade Dragon Caper</code>, <code>Howard Duff Sam Spade</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Jade Dragon Caper (Starring Howard Duff as Sam Spade — Wildroot)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1947-08-24<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Wildroot Co.</strong><br>Wildroot Cream-Oil Hair Tonic</td>\n<td><strong>Howard Duff</strong> (Sam Spade), <strong>Lurene Tuttle</strong> (Effie)</td>\n<td><strong>Dashiell Hammett</strong>, <strong>Bob Tallman</strong>, <strong>William Spier</strong>, <strong>Dick Joy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Private investigator Sam Spade is hired by an elusive art collector to recover an ancient, supposedly cursed carved jade dragon stolen from an ocean liner stateroom, leading him into a lethal showdown with two rival underworld fences along the foggy San Francisco docks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ethel_Albert_1957_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Recipe Arithmetic</code>, <code>Peg Lynch Alan Bunce</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Recipe Arithmetic &amp; The Cookbook (Peg Lynch &amp; Alan Bunce — NBC Monitor)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Monitor</strong><br>1957-08-24<br>11:20 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Monitor Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Peg Lynch</strong> (Ethel), <strong>Alan Bunce</strong> (Albert)</td>\n<td><strong>Peg Lynch</strong> (Creator/Writer), <strong>Frank Blair</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Humor&quot;, &quot;Marriage&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Ethel attempts to double a complex casserole recipe for a dinner party with the neighbors, leading to an uproarious domestic argument with Albert over fractional mathematics, cup measurements, and baking temperatures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Human_Adv_1940_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Story Of Chicago</code>, <code>Chicago Frontier to Metropolis</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Story of Chicago: Frontier to Metropolis (University of Chicago Drama)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WGN)</strong><br>1940-08-24<br>8:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>University of Chicago</td>\n<td><strong>John McIntire</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Frank Readick</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mitchell Grayson &amp; Sherman Dryer</strong>, <strong>Ken Nordine</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Documentary&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Urban&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Produced in cooperation with the University of Chicago, this sweeping historical docudrama traces Chicago&#39;s extraordinary rise from a remote frontier swamp settlement (Fort Dearborn) into a towering industrial metropolis following the Great Fire of 1871.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 102</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
