    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 11)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, British BBC Third Programme, and syndication broadcasting history (1950–1996):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 11)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Whatever_Became_1971_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Lon McAllister</code>, <code>Richard Lamparski Lon McCallister</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Whatever Became Of: Lon McCallister (Richard Lamparski Interview)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WBAI / Pacifica</strong><br>1971-08-24<br>(Pacifica Syndication)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Cultural Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Richard Lamparski</strong> (Host), <strong>Lon McCallister</strong> (Guest)</td>\n<td><strong>Richard Lamparski</strong> (Producer/Biographer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Biographical&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Hollywood&quot;, &quot;Nostalgia&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Biographer Richard Lamparski interviews former Hollywood heartthrob and WWII juvenile star Lon McCallister, discussing his rapid rise at 20th Century Fox, his transition into real estate, and life after retirement from the screen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Maisie_1950_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Orphanage</code>, <code>Maisie Ann Sothern 1950</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Orphanage Benefit (Starring Ann Sothern as Maisie Ravier — MGM)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>MGM Syndication</strong><br>1950-08-24<br>(Radio Attractions)</td>\n<td><strong>Regional Advertisers</strong><br>Department Stores</td>\n<td><strong>Ann Sothern</strong> (Maisie), <strong>Pat McGeehan</strong>, <strong>Lurene Tuttle</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Arthur Phillips</strong> (Writer), <strong>John Lake</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Humor&quot;, &quot;Human Interest&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Brass-hearted showgirl Maisie Ravier volunteers at a local children&#39;s orphanage to organize a charity talent carnival, helping an insecure young boy find a loving adoptive home despite meddlesome board members.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Life_Hands_1951_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Midnight Intruder</code>, <code>Lee Bowman Gardner</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Midnight Intruder (Starring Lee Bowman — Erle Stanley Gardner)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1951-08-24<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Mystery Series</td>\n<td><strong>Lee Bowman</strong> (Jonathan Kring), <strong>Amzie Strickland</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Erle Stanley Gardner</strong>, <strong>Larry Marcus</strong>, <strong>Kenneth Banghart</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Courtroom&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Legal&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Created by Erle Stanley Gardner, brilliant defense attorney Jonathan Kring cross-examines key eyewitnesses in a murder trial, using ballistics angles and a broken wristwatch to prove a young mechanic was framed for shooting his foreman.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Canterbury_1950_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Franklin&#39;s Tale</code>, <code>Nevill Coghill Chaucer</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Franklin&#39;s Tale (Geoffrey Chaucer — BBC Third Programme)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Third Prog.</strong><br>1950-08-24<br>8:00 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Cultural Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Cecil Trouncer</strong> (The Franklin), <strong>Marius Goring</strong>, <strong>Jill Balcon</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Geoffrey Chaucer</strong>, <strong>Nevill Coghill</strong>, <strong>Antony Hopkins</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Medieval&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Poetry&quot;, &quot;British&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Nevill Coghill&#39;s acclaimed verse translation of Geoffrey Chaucer&#39;s <em>The Franklin&#39;s Tale</em>, exploring noble chivalry, rash promises, and honor in Brittany as a devoted knight releases his faithful wife from an impossible vow.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mystery_Project_1996_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Black Museum</code>, <code>Jim French Mystery Project</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Black Museum: Modern Echoes (Jim French&#39;s Mystery Project)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jim French Prods.</strong><br>1996-08-24<br>(KIXI Seattle)</td>\n<td><strong>Regional Advertisers</strong><br>Washington Sponsors</td>\n<td><strong>Larry Albert</strong>, <strong>Pat French</strong>, <strong>John Gilbert</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jim French</strong> (Writer/Director), <strong>Bill Shurtliff</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Modern Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Jim French&#39;s contemporary audio thriller anthology, an American tourist visiting Scotland Yard&#39;s macabre Black Museum becomes entangled in a modern re-enactment of an unsolved Victorian poison mystery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Tall_Tales_1954_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Handcuffs John L Sullivan</code>, <code>Arthur Q Bryan Uncle Billy</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Handcuffs for John L. Sullivan (Arthur Q. Bryan — Tall Tales)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mastertone Trans.</strong><br>1954-08-24<br>(Feature #12)</td>\n<td><strong>Regional Advertisers</strong><br>Flour &amp; Bakery Sponsors</td>\n<td><strong>Arthur Q. Bryan</strong> (Uncle Billy), <strong>Frank Nelson</strong>, <strong>Herb Vigran</strong></td>\n<td><strong>George W. Allen</strong> (Writer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Folklore&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Tall Tale&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Uncle Billy spins an uproarious tall yarn about a boastful frontier sheriff who claimed he could put the cuffs on heavyweight boxing champion John L. Sullivan during the bare-knuckle legend&#39;s exhibition tour through the Montana gold camps.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 66</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
