    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 10)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, AFRS military, and syndication broadcasting history (1935–1952):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 10)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Rocky_Jordan_1952_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Man They All Loved</code>, <code>Rocky Jordan Cairo 1952</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Man They All Loved (Starring George Trench as Rocky Jordan)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1952-08-24<br>6:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>CBS Adventure Series</td>\n<td><strong>George Trench</strong> (Rocky Jordan), <strong>Jay Novello</strong>, <strong>Virginia Gregg</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Larry Roman</strong> (Writer), <strong>Cliff Howell</strong>, <strong>Larry Thor</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Exotic&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In the shadow of the minarets at the Cafe Tambourine in Cairo, American expatriate Rocky Jordan is drawn into a lethal intrigue involving the murder of a charismatic British archaeologist whom three different women claim was their lawful husband.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Flash_Gordon_1935_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Azura Regains Memory</code>, <code>Flash Gordon Chapter 18</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Azura Regains Memory (Chapter 18 — Starring Gale Gordon as Flash Gordon)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Hearst Syndication</strong><br>1935-08-24<br>(Chapter 18)</td>\n<td><strong>Regional Advertisers</strong><br>Comic Strip Sponsors</td>\n<td><strong>Gale Gordon</strong> (Flash Gordon), <strong>Maurice Franklin</strong>, <strong>Bruno Wick</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Alex Raymond</strong> (Creator), <strong>Himan Brown</strong>, <strong>Harry Crocker</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Space Opera&quot;, &quot;Fantasy&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>On planet Mongo, Queen Azura regains her lost memory after Flash Gordon and Dr. Zarkov disable the Witch Queen&#39;s hypnotic mind-control crystal, leading to an open rebellion against the tyranny of Emperor Ming the Merciless.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mail_Call_1943_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Guest - Groucho Marx</code>, <code>Groucho Duke Ellington 1943</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Mail Call #53: Groucho Marx, Betty Hutton &amp; Duke Ellington (AFRS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Unit</strong><br>1943-08-24<br>(Show #53)</td>\n<td><strong>U.S. War Dept</strong><br>Armed Forces Radio Service</td>\n<td><strong>Groucho Marx</strong> (Host), <strong>Betty Hutton</strong>, <strong>Duke Ellington Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood Unit</strong>, <strong>Don Wilson</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Morale&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of quick-witted ad-libs Groucho Marx hosts wartime <em>Mail Call</em> from Hollywood for American troops abroad, trading frantic comedy with energetic dynamo Betty Hutton and swinging to Duke Ellington&#39;s Orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>To_Rear_1946_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>AFRS Collection</code>, <code>Burgess Meredith Housing</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;To the Rear, March! Veterans Housing &amp; G.I. Bill Special (Burgess Meredith)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Unit</strong><br>1946-08-24<br>(Series Finale)</td>\n<td><strong>U.S. War Dept</strong><br>Armed Forces Radio Service</td>\n<td><strong>Burgess Meredith</strong> (Host), <strong>Mel Blanc</strong>, <strong>Frank Nelson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jerome Lawrence &amp; Robert E. Lee</strong>, <strong>Hy Averback</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Host Burgess Meredith guides returning WWII veterans through the intricacies of veteran home-loan applications, G.I. Bill tuition programs, and civilian job placement, punctuated by comedic character vignettes from Mel Blanc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Spotlight_Bands_1944_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Somebody Loves Me</code>, <code>Harry James Corpus Christi</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Spotlight Bands #602: Harry James from Naval Air Station Corpus Christi (Coca-Cola)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Network (WJZ)</strong><br>1944-08-24<br>9:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Coca-Cola Co.</strong><br>&quot;Have a Coke&quot; / Morale</td>\n<td><strong>Harry James &amp; Music Makers</strong>, <strong>Buddy DiVito</strong>, <strong>Kitty Kallen</strong></td>\n<td><strong>D&#39;Arcy Advertising</strong>, <strong>Gil Martyn</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Morale&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Direct from the Naval Air Station in Corpus Christi, Texas, trumpet king Harry James and his Music Makers ignite military service personnel with roaring swing renditions of &#39;Somebody Loves Me&#39; and &#39;Two O&#39;Clock Jump&#39; for Coca-Cola.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Big_Story_1949_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dead Illusion Marie Wathen</code>, <code>Marie Wathen Big Story</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;A Dead Illusion Can Destroy: Marie Wathen of The Montgomery Advertiser (Pall Mall)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1949-08-24<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>American Cigarette Co.</strong><br>Pall Mall Famous Cigarettes</td>\n<td><strong>Robert Sloane</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Joan Alexander</strong>, <strong>Bill Lipton</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bernard J. Prockter</strong> (Producer), <strong>Ernest Chappell</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;True Crime&quot;, &quot;Journalism&quot;, &quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Investigative&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The true journalistic investigation of Alabama reporter Marie Wathen of <em>The Montgomery Advertiser</em>, who unraveled a web of small-town perjury and corrupt framing to save an innocent man sentenced to death for armed robbery.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 60</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
