    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 61)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across British BBC and American network broadcasting history (1935–1997):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 61)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1969_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Flowering Cherry</code>, <code>Robert Bolt BBC 1969</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Flowering Cherry (Starring Brenda Bruce &amp; Andrew Cruickshank — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1969-08-23<br>8:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Brenda Bruce</strong> (Isobel), <strong>Andrew Cruickshank</strong> (Jim), <strong>David Spenser</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Robert Bolt</strong> (Playwright), <strong>Norman Wright</strong>, <strong>Ronald Fletcher</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;Theater&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Robert Bolt&#39;s poignant domestic tragedy, an insurance clerk lives in a self-deluding fantasy of retiring to a Somerset fruit orchard, but his inability to confront reality slowly fractures his family and destroys his marriage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1975_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Witch Wood</code>, <code>John Buchan Scottish Drama</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Witch Wood (Starring David Hayman &amp; Tom Fleming — BBC Radio 4)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1975-08-23<br>8:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>David Hayman</strong> (David Semphill), <strong>Tom Fleming</strong>, <strong>Edith MacArthur</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Buchan</strong> (Author), <strong>Donald Campbell</strong>, <strong>Gordon Emslie</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;Scottish&quot;, &quot;Horror&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In 17th-century Scotland, newly ordained minister David Semphill discovers that respected elders of his rigid Presbyterian parish are secretly practicing dark pagan rituals in the ancient primeval wood of Melanudrigill.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1980_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Events Black Tor</code>, <code>Roy Clarke BBC 1980</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Events at Black Tor (Starring John Castle &amp; Michael Gough — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1980-08-23<br>8:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>John Castle</strong>, <strong>Barbara Leigh-Hunt</strong>, <strong>Michael Gough</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Roy Clarke</strong> (Writer), <strong>Brian Miller</strong>, <strong>Dick Mills</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A forensic archaeologist investigating a series of unexplained livestock deaths and pagan symbols near a remote Dartmoor stone tor uncovers a terrifying modern conspiracy tied to ancient sacrificial folklore.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1986_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Leave It to Psmith</code>, <code>Jonathan Cecil Wodehouse</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Leave It to Psmith (Starring Jonathan Cecil &amp; Ian Carmichael — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1986-08-23<br>8:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Jonathan Cecil</strong> (Psmith), <strong>Ian Carmichael</strong>, <strong>Moray Watson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>P.G. Wodehouse</strong> (Author), <strong>Michael Bakewell</strong>, <strong>John Tydeman</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Humor&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Adaptation&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In P.G. Wodehouse&#39;s classic farce, suave and monocled gentleman of leisure Ronald Psmith places an advertisement offering his services for &#39;any enterprise not involving manual work&#39;, becoming embroiled in a diamond theft scheme at Blandings Castle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1997_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Problem of Thor Bridge</code>, <code>Clive Merrison Holmes</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Problem of Thor Bridge (Starring Clive Merrison &amp; Michael Williams as Holmes &amp; Watson)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1997-08-23<br>2:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Clive Merrison</strong> (Sherlock Holmes), <strong>Michael Williams</strong> (Watson)</td>\n<td><strong>Sir Arthur Conan Doyle</strong> (Author), <strong>Bert Coules</strong>, <strong>Patrick Rayner</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Victorian&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Adaptation&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson investigate the baffling gunshot murder of an American Gold King&#39;s wife on the parapet of Thor Bridge, uncovering a fiendishly clever suicidal trap designed to frame the innocent governess.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Beauty_Box_1935_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Countess Maritza</code>, <code>Gladys Swarthout Palmolive</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Countess Maritza (Starring Gladys Swarthout &amp; Frank Chapman — Palmolive Soap)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1935-08-23<br>10:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive</strong><br>Palmolive Soap</td>\n<td><strong>Gladys Swarthout</strong> (Countess Maritza), <strong>Frank Chapman</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Emmerich Kálmán</strong>, <strong>Kenneth MacGregor</strong>, <strong>Nathaniel Shilkret</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Operetta&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Metropolitan Opera mezzo-soprano Gladys Swarthout stars in Emmerich Kálmán&#39;s romantic operetta <em>Countess Maritza</em>, singing passionate Hungarian csárdás and romantic waltzes alongside baritone Frank Chapman for Palmolive Soap.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 366</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
