    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 41)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American network and educational broadcasting history (1936–1950):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 41)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Mr_Chameleon_1950_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Want Ad Murder</code>, <code>Karl Swenson Chameleon</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Want Ad Murder Case (Starring Karl Swenson — Ironized Yeast)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1950-08-23<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sterling Drug Inc.</strong><br>Ironized Yeast &amp; Campho-Phenique</td>\n<td><strong>Karl Swenson</strong> (Mr. Chameleon), <strong>Frank Butler</strong>, <strong>Roger De Koven</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frank &amp; Anne Hummert</strong> (Producers), <strong>Howard Claney</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Police Procedural&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of disguise Mr. Chameleon adopts the undercover persona of an eccentric antique book collector to trap a ruthless killer who lured wealthy victims through fraudulent classified newspaper want-ads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Alan_Young_1944_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Alan Young Lifeguard</code>, <code>Alan Young Sal Hepatica</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Alan Young as a Hollywood Beach Lifeguard (Sal Hepatica &amp; Vitalis)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1944-08-23<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Bristol-Myers Co.</strong><br>Sal Hepatica &amp; Vitalis Hair Tonic</td>\n<td><strong>Alan Young</strong>, <strong>Jean Gillespie</strong>, <strong>Jim Ameche</strong>, <strong>Lud Gluskin Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Eddie Pola</strong> (Director), <strong>Jimmy Wallington</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Befuddled young comedian Alan Young tries his hand at becoming a glamorous Hollywood lifeguard to impress his girlfriend, sparking beachside misadventures and satirical songs backed by Lud Gluskin&#39;s Orchestra for Sal Hepatica.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Research_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Weaver And Wasp</code>, <code>Westinghouse Science 1949</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Weaver and the Wasp: History of Paper (Westinghouse Science)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KDKA / Syndication</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>(Transcribed)</td>\n<td><strong>Westinghouse Electric</strong><br>Public Service Science Series</td>\n<td><strong>Dr. Phillips Thomas</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Paul Shannon</strong> (Host)</td>\n<td><strong>Charles A. Scarlott</strong> (Writer), Westinghouse Labs</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Science&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dr. Phillips Thomas explores the history of human papermaking and natural biomimicry, explaining how 18th-century French scientist René Antoine de Réaumur discovered modern wood-pulp paper manufacturing by observing paper wasps.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Magic_Key_1936_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Seeing America San Francisco</code>, <code>Gladys Swarthout RCA</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Magic Key of RCA: Gladys Swarthout &amp; Frank Black&#39;s Symphony (RCA Victor)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1936-08-23<br>2:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Radio Corp of America</strong><br>RCA Victor Radios &amp; Records</td>\n<td><strong>Gladys Swarthout</strong>, <strong>Frank Black &amp; NBC Symphony Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Thomas L. Stix</strong> (Producer), <strong>Milton Cross</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dr. Frank Black conducts the NBC Symphony Orchestra in a coast-to-coast variety showcase for RCA Victor, featuring Metropolitan Opera star Gladys Swarthout and a live shortwave musical remote from San Francisco&#39;s Golden Gate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Family_Theater_1950_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>20000 Leagues Under Sea</code>, <code>Macdonald Carey Nemo</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Jules Verne&#39;s 20,000 Leagues Under the Sea (Hosted by Roddy McDowall)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (KHJ)</strong><br>1950-08-23<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Family Rosary Crusade</td>\n<td><strong>Roddy McDowall</strong> (Host), <strong>Macdonald Carey</strong> (Nemo), <strong>Bill Johnstone</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jules Verne</strong> (Author), <strong>Jaime del Valle</strong>, <strong>Tony La Frano</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Host Roddy McDowall presents Jules Verne&#39;s underwater epic <em>Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea</em>, starring Macdonald Carey as the enigmatic Captain Nemo commanding the submarine <em>Nautilus</em> on a voyage into the ocean depths.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Authors_Playhouse_1942_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>I Give My Life</code>, <code>Arch Oboler Authors Playhouse</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Arch Oboler&#39;s &#39;I Give My Life&#39; (Starring Bret Morrison &amp; Marvin Miller)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1942-08-23<br>6:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Drama Anthology</td>\n<td><strong>Bret Morrison</strong>, <strong>Marvin Miller</strong>, <strong>Betty Winkler</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Arch Oboler</strong> (Author), <strong>Wynn Wright</strong>, <strong>Fort Pearson</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;War&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In a wartime psychological drama written by Arch Oboler, an underground resistance saboteur in Nazi-occupied Europe risks everything to save an innocent village from brutal reprisal, weighing his personal survival against the sacrifice for freedom.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 246</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
