    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 27)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American and British broadcasting history (1942–1980):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 27)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Quiz_Kids_1942_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Quiz Kids 113</code>, <code>Ruth Duskin Shakespeare</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Quiz Kids #113: Joe Kelly with Richard Williams &amp; Ruth Duskin (Alka-Seltzer)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Network</strong><br>1942-08-23<br>8:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Miles Laboratories</strong><br>Alka-Seltzer &amp; One-A-Day</td>\n<td><strong>Joe Kelly</strong> (Quizmaster), <strong>Richard Williams</strong>, <strong>Harve Fischman</strong>, <strong>Ruth Duskin</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Louis G. Cowan</strong> (Creator), <strong>Fort Pearson</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Game Show&quot;, &quot;Quiz&quot;, &quot;Children&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Quizmaster Joe Kelly tests child prodigies Richard Williams, Harve Fischman, and 8-year-old Ruth Duskin with listeners&#39; brainteasers on classical literature, European war geography, and quadratic mathematics for Alka-Seltzer war savings bonds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Comic_Weekly_1953_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bringing Up Father Jiggs</code>, <code>Puck Comic Weekly 1953</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Comic Weekly Man: Reading &#39;Bringing Up Father&#39; &amp; &#39;Blondie&#39; (Puck Comic Weekly)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WOR New York</strong><br>1953-08-23<br>9:00 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Puck – The Comic Weekly</strong><br>N.Y. Journal-American</td>\n<td><strong>The Comic Weekly Man</strong> (Dramatic Reader)</td>\n<td><strong>King Features Syndicate</strong>, New York</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Children&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Comic Strips&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The Comic Weekly Man reads the Sunday color comics aloud for children, performing character voices for George McManus&#39;s <em>Bringing Up Father</em> with Jiggs and Maggie, <em>Blondie</em>, <em>Prince Valiant</em>, and <em>The Little King</em>, opening with a bicycle riddle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Raymond_Scott_1944_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Sweet and Lovely</code>, <code>Dorothy Collins Raymond Scott</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Raymond Scott and his Orchestra with Dorothy Collins Singing &#39;Sweet and Lovely&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1944-08-23<br>11:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>CBS Jazz Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Scott</strong> (Piano / Leader), <strong>Dorothy Collins</strong> (Vocalist)</td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Scott</strong> (Arranger), <strong>Warren Sweeney</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Innovator Raymond Scott leads his full modern jazz orchestra in dynamic swing arrangements from CBS New York, featuring &#39;Sweet and Lovely&#39;, Scott&#39;s signature descriptive jazz instrumental &#39;Powerhouse&#39;, and vocals by Dorothy Collins.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Goon_Show_1951_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Crazy People 13</code>, <code>Spike Milligan Bentine 1951</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Goon Show: Crazy People (Series 1, Episode 13 — Spike Milligan &amp; Peter Sellers)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Home Service</strong><br>1951-08-23<br>9:00 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Public Radio</td>\n<td><strong>Spike Milligan</strong>, <strong>Peter Sellers</strong>, <strong>Harry Secombe</strong>, <strong>Michael Bentine</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Larry Stephens &amp; Spike Milligan</strong> (Writers), <strong>Dennis Main Wilson</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Surrealism&quot;, &quot;British&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The original founding quartet of Goons—Spike Milligan, Peter Sellers, Harry Secombe, and Michael Bentine—present surreal sketch comedy and vocal parody routines in the historic first series of <em>Crazy People</em> on the BBC Home Service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Haunted_1980_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Late Departure</code>, <code>Haunted Series 1 Ep 6</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Haunted: The Late Departure (Roy Russell Ghost Story)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1980-08-23<br>10:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Nigel Anthony</strong> (Arthur), <strong>Steve Hodson</strong>, <strong>Elizabeth Bell</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Roy Russell</strong> (Writer), <strong>Vanessa Whitburn</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;British&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A weary late-night commuter waiting for a delayed connection at a desolate railway junction experiences terrifying visions of a tragic train disaster that occurred on the same platform fifty years earlier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Our_Miss_Brooks_1948_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Swiss Chronometer Watch</code>, <code>Connie Brooks Palmolive</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Swiss Chronometer Watch Auction (Palmolive &amp; Lustre-Creme)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX)</strong><br>1948-08-23<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive</strong><br>Palmolive Soap &amp; Lustre-Creme</td>\n<td><strong>Eve Arden</strong> (Connie Brooks), <strong>Jeff Chandler</strong>, <strong>Gale Gordon</strong>, <strong>Richard Crenna</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Al Lewis</strong> (Creator &amp; Director), <strong>Verne Smith</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;School&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Madison High English teacher Connie Brooks tries to impress biology teacher Philip Boynton by bidding on a fancy Swiss chronometer wristwatch at an estate auction, only to get into a bidding war against principal Osgood Conklin.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 162</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
