    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 24)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American broadcasting history (1943–1953):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 24)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Spotlight_Bands_1943_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Chester Bounce</code>, <code>Fort Belvoir Spotlight</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Spotlight Bands #597: Bob Chester Live from Fort Belvoir (Coca-Cola)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Network</strong><br>1943-08-23<br>9:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Coca-Cola Co.</strong><br>Coca-Cola</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Chester and his Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Betty Bradley</strong>, <strong>Gene Howard</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Larry Hammond</strong> (Director), <strong>Gil Martyn</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Bob Chester and his Orchestra broadcast live from the Engineer Replacement Training Center at Fort Belvoir, Virginia, performing swing favorites for thousands of servicemen, kicking off with &#39;The Bounce&#39; and featuring Betty Bradley singing &#39;You&#39;ll Never Know&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Spotlight_Bands_1944_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Flying Telegraph</code>, <code>Bobby Sherwood Camp Kearns</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Spotlight Bands #911: Bobby Sherwood Live from Camp Kearns (Coca-Cola)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Network</strong><br>1944-08-23<br>9:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Coca-Cola Co.</strong><br>Coca-Cola</td>\n<td><strong>Bobby Sherwood and his Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Gil Martyn</strong> (Announcer), D&#39;Arcy Advertising</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Bobby Sherwood and his Orchestra bring high-energy swing to troops stationed at the Army Air Forces Overseas Replacement Depot in Camp Kearns, Utah, spotlighting their signature showstopper &#39;The Elks&#39; Parade&#39; and hot jump tune &#39;The Flying Telegraph&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AFRS_Spotlight_1944_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bobby Sherwood AFRS</code>, <code>Spotlight Bands 431</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;AFRS Spotlight Bands #431: Bobby Sherwood and his Orchestra&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood</strong><br>1944-08-23<br>(AFRS Disc #431)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>War Dept GI Broadcast</td>\n<td><strong>Bobby Sherwood and his Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Production</strong>, Blue Network Masters</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Armed Forces Radio Service presents the overseas GI broadcast of Bobby Sherwood and his Orchestra, playing fast-paced swing arrangements and jump blues for soldiers stationed across the European and Pacific battlefronts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Heres_To_Romance_1945_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Patrice Munsel Evening in Paris</code>, <code>Larry Douglas 1945</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Here&#39;s to Romance: Larry Douglas with Guest Patrice Munsel (Evening in Paris)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1945-08-23<br>10:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Bourjois, Inc.</strong><br>Evening in Paris Perfume &amp; Powder</td>\n<td><strong>Larry Douglas</strong> (Tenor), <strong>Patrice Munsel</strong>, <strong>Ray Bloch and his Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Marx Loeb</strong> (Director), <strong>Frank Gallop</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Tenor Larry Douglas and maestro Ray Bloch welcome Metropolitan Opera star soprano Patrice Munsel for a romantic musical program sponsored by Evening in Paris perfume, singing operatic duets and classic love ballads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>I_Was_A_Communist_1953_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Red Infiltration Steel</code>, <code>Matt Cvetic Pittsburgh</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Red Infiltration of Steel (Starring Dana Andrews as Matt Cvetic)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ziv Syndication</strong><br>1953-08-23<br>(WNBC / Syndicated)</td>\n<td><strong>Local Commercials</strong><br>Co-op Advertisers</td>\n<td><strong>Dana Andrews</strong> (Matt Cvetic), <strong>Ken Peters</strong>, <strong>Joy Terry</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Henry Hayward</strong> (Director), <strong>David Rose</strong>, <strong>Fred Foy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Spy&quot;, &quot;Espionage&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Cold War&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Undercover FBI operative Matt Cvetic infiltrates a Pittsburgh Communist Party cell plotting industrial sabotage at a steel manufacturing plant, risking exposure as a traitor to his family while feeding counter-espionage intelligence to federal agents.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Frank_Sinatra_1944_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Sinatra Vimms 1944</code>, <code>Sinatra Axel Stordahl</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Frank Sinatra Show with Axel Stordahl and his Orchestra (Vimms Vitamins)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX)</strong><br>1944-08-23<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Lever Brothers Co.</strong><br>Vimms Multi-Vitamins</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Sinatra</strong>, <strong>Eileen Barton</strong>, <strong>Bobby Tucker Singers</strong>, <strong>Axel Stordahl Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>William N. Robson</strong> (Director), <strong>Harlow Wilcox</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Frank Sinatra thrills swooning bobby-soxers and wartime radio listeners on CBS, crooning &#39;Long Ago and Far Away&#39;, &#39;I&#39;ll Be Seeing You&#39;, and &#39;The Music Stopped&#39;, backed by the lush arrangements of Axel Stordahl and his Orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 144</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
