    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 18)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American and South African broadcasting history (1934–1954):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 18)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Command_Perf_1945_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tone Whiting Davis</code>, <code>Command Performance 191</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Command Performance #191 (Franchot Tone, Margaret Whiting &amp; Joan Davis)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood</strong><br>1945-08-23<br>(AFRS Disc #191)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>War Dept Troop Radio</td>\n<td><strong>Franchot Tone</strong> (MC), <strong>Margaret Whiting</strong>, <strong>Joan Davis</strong>, <strong>King&#39;s Men</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lou Bring</strong> (Music Director), AFRS Hollywood</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of ceremonies Franchot Tone welcomes vocal sensation Margaret Whiting and comedy star Joan Davis for GI troop requests, featuring Whiting singing &#39;Moonlight in Vermont&#39; and Davis performing an uproarious comedy routine about dating post-war servicemen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Command_Perf_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Ferrer Garrett Modernaires</code>, <code>Command Performance 394</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Command Performance #394 (José Ferrer, Betty Garrett &amp; The Modernaires)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>(AFRS Disc #394)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>Armed Forces Radio</td>\n<td><strong>José Ferrer</strong> (MC), <strong>Betty Garrett</strong>, <strong>The Modernaires</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Meredith Willson</strong> (Music Director), <strong>Hy Averback</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of ceremonies José Ferrer leads an energetic GI variety hour with Broadway songstress Betty Garrett and The Modernaires, featuring Garrett singing &#39;There&#39;s No Business Like Show Business&#39; and Ferrer performing a humorous dramatic monologue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Grey_Goose_1953_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Diamonds Skeleton Coast</code>, <code>Captain Grey Pegasus</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Diamonds of the Skeleton Coast (Pegasus Motor Oil)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Springbok Radio</strong><br>1953-08-23<br>8:00 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Mobil Oil South Africa</strong><br>Pegasus Motor Oil</td>\n<td><strong>Michael Silver</strong> (Captain Grey), <strong>Gordon Mulholland</strong>, <strong>Diane Appleby</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Michael Silver</strong> (Creator &amp; Writer), SABC Johannesburg</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;International&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Adventurer Captain Grey sails the schooner <em>Grey Goose</em> along South Africa&#39;s treacherous Skeleton Coast, outmaneuvering diamond smugglers to recover a sunken crate of industrial gems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Watanabe_Archie_1934_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Frank Watanabe and Archie</code>, <code>The Stolen Blueprints</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Misplaced Estate Blueprints (Eddie Holden &amp; Reginald Sharland)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KNX Los Angeles</strong><br>1934-08-23<br>7:15 PM PST</td>\n<td><strong>Southern Pacific</strong><br>Pacific Advertisers</td>\n<td><strong>Eddie Holden</strong> (Frank Watanabe), <strong>Reginald Sharland</strong> (Archie)</td>\n<td><strong>Eddie Holden &amp; Reginald Sharland</strong> (Writers)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Houseboy Frank Watanabe and his eccentric British employer the Honorable Archie become accidentally embroiled in a comedic neighborhood misunderstanding when Archie misplaces important estate blueprints in a laundry hamper.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Big_City_Serenade_1952_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Salute To Duluth</code>, <code>Duluth Lake Superior</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;A Musical Salute to Duluth and Lake Superior (Alexander Courage)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1952-08-23<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Music Bureau</td>\n<td><strong>Alexander Courage</strong> (Conductor), <strong>NBC Strings</strong>, <strong>Skip Farrell</strong>, <strong>Mary Ellen</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Wayne Howell</strong> (Announcer), NBC Radio</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Orchestral&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Alexander Courage conducts the NBC orchestra in an atmospheric musical salute to Duluth, Minnesota and Lake Superior, performing lush orchestral arrangements of American regional melodies and popular ballads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Dinner_Bell_1954_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Last Episode Of Series</code>, <code>Dinner Bell Final Broadcast</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Dinner Bell Roundup Time Series Finale with Arthur C. Page &amp; Lulu Belle and Scotty&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WLS Chicago</strong><br>1954-08-23<br>12:00 PM CDT</td>\n<td><strong>Moorman Mfg. Co.</strong><br>MoorMan&#39;s Mintrates</td>\n<td><strong>Arthur C. Page</strong> (Host), <strong>Lulu Belle and Scotty</strong>, <strong>The Prairie Ramblers</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Chuck Acree</strong> (Announcer), <strong>Ralph Emerson</strong> (Organ)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Folk&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Agriculture&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Broadcaster Arthur C. Page presents the series finale of <em>Dinner Bell Roundup Time</em> live from WLS Chicago, sharing Midwestern crop reports and farm community retrospectives alongside traditional folk harmonies from Lulu Belle and Scotty.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 108</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
