    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American, British, and public broadcasting history (1936–2000):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Eternal_Light_1971_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Immigrant&#39;s Dream</code>, <code>Lower East Side 1971</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Eternal Light: The Immigrant&#39;s Dream on the Lower East Side&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1971-08-22<br>12:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Jewish Theological</strong><br>Seminary of America</td>\n<td><strong>Norman Rose</strong> (Narrator), dramatic radio ensemble</td>\n<td><strong>Morton Wishengrad</strong> (Writer), <strong>Daniel Sutter</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Inspirational&quot;, &quot;Religious&quot;, &quot;Cultural&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Norman Rose narrates a moving biographical dramatization exploring the life of an impoverished Eastern European immigrant tailor who arrives on Manhattan&#39;s Lower East Side, establishing a community mutual aid society to support orphanages and labor education.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Al_Jolson_1936_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tallulah Bankhead Jolson</code>, <code>Rinso Trocadero 1936</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Al Jolson with Guest Tallulah Bankhead, Martha Raye, and Parkyakarkus&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1936-08-22<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive-Peet</strong><br>Rinso Detergent &amp; Lifebuoy Soap</td>\n<td><strong>Al Jolson</strong>, <strong>Tallulah Bankhead</strong>, <strong>Parkyakarkus</strong>, <strong>Martha Raye</strong>, <strong>Victor Young Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Harry Tugend</strong> (Writer), <strong>Joseph R. Stauffer</strong> (Director), <strong>Tiny Ruffner</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Broadway legend Al Jolson trades sharp-tongued banter with flamboyant stage queen Tallulah Bankhead, jokes with Greek dialect comedian Parkyakarkus, and belts out musical classics including &#39;Swanee&#39; and &#39;My Mammy&#39; alongside Martha Raye for Rinso.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2000X_2000_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Shambleau Bloodchild</code>, <code>Octavia Butler CL Moore</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Shambleau (by C.L. Moore) &amp; Bloodchild (by Octavia Butler)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NPR Satellite</strong><br>2000-08-22<br>(2000X Series)</td>\n<td><strong>CPB / NPR</strong><br>Corporation for Public Broadcasting</td>\n<td><strong>Richard Dreyfuss</strong> (Host), <strong>Armin Shimerman</strong>, <strong>Robin Atkin Downes</strong>, <strong>Gates McFadden</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Erik Bauersfeld &amp; Yuri Rasovsky</strong> (Adapters), <strong>Jim McKee</strong> (Sound Design)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Richard Dreyfuss introduces a provocative science-fiction double feature dramatizing C.L. Moore&#39;s classic <em>Shambleau</em>—where Martian smuggler Northwest Smith rescues an alluring alien creature with deadly psychic tendrils—followed by Octavia Butler&#39;s Hugo-winning <em>Bloodchild</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Crime_Doctor_1943_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Forged Checkbook</code>, <code>Dr Ordway Parole</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Forged Checkbook (Parole Board Hearing)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1943-08-22<br>8:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Philip Morris &amp; Co.</strong><br>&quot;Call for Philip Morris!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Ray Collins</strong> (Dr. Ordway), <strong>Everett Sloane</strong>, <strong>Alice Frost</strong>, <strong>Santos Ortega</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Max Marcin</strong> (Creator), <strong>Jack Johnstone</strong> (Director), <strong>Ken Roberts</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Medical&quot;, &quot;Psychological Thriller&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Criminal psychologist Dr. Benjamin Ordway investigates a paroled bank accountant accused of forging company payroll checks, uncovering psychological evidence that the accountant&#39;s embittered brother-in-law planted the forged signature stamps to trigger a parole violation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Murder_Experts_1949_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Heavy Hangs Head</code>, <code>Guillotine Curse Murder</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Heavy Hangs the Head (Hosted by John Dickson Carr)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1949-08-22<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Mystery Writers of America</strong><br>Sustaining Mystery Series</td>\n<td><strong>John Dickson Carr</strong> (Guest Host), <strong>Lawson Zerbe</strong>, <strong>Donald Buka</strong>, <strong>Elspeth Eric</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Robert A. Arthur &amp; David Kogan</strong> (Creators), <strong>David Kogan</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Renowned locked-room mystery author John Dickson Carr presents a macabre tale of an eccentric French antique collector who is decapitated by an antique parlor guillotine inside a bolted study, challenging listeners to untangle the ingenious mechanical contraption used by the heir.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_Theatre_1953_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>London Assurance</code>, <code>Dion Boucicault Victorian</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;London Assurance (by Dion Boucicault, Starring Jack Hulbert &amp; Cicely Courtneidge)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Home Service</strong><br>1953-08-22<br>9:15 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>British Broadcasting Corp.</td>\n<td><strong>Jack Hulbert</strong>, <strong>Cicely Courtneidge</strong>, <strong>David Tomlinson</strong>, <strong>Frank Pettingell</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Dion Boucicault</strong> (Author), <strong>Cynthia Pughe</strong> (Adapter), <strong>Archie Campbell</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Theatre&quot;, &quot;Victorian&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Dion Boucicault&#39;s witty Victorian comedy of manners, aging London dandy Sir Harcourt Courtly arrives at an aristocratic Gloucestershire estate to wed an eighteen-year-old heiress, only for his spendthrift son Charles to arrive disguised under a false name and steal her affections.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 212</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
