    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American network and syndicated broadcasting history (1943–2004):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Sherlock_Holmes_2004_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Master Craftsman</code>, <code>Clockmaker of Clerkenwell</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Master Craftsman&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Imagination Theatre</strong><br>2004-08-22<br>(Syndicated)</td>\n<td><strong>National Audio Drama Syndication</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Patrick Lowrie</strong> (Sherlock Holmes), <strong>John Armstrong</strong> (Dr. Watson), <strong>Rick May</strong> (Lestrade), <strong>Dennis Bateman</strong> (Miles)</td>\n<td><strong>M.J. Elliott</strong> (Writer), <strong>Jim French</strong> (Director), <strong>Terry Rose</strong> (Music)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson investigate the poisoning of a master clockmaker in Clerkenwell whose custom-built celestial clock holds the combination to an uncrackable Bank of England vault, deducing that the murderer used a spring-loaded poisoned escapement wheel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gene_Autry_1948_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Buttons and Bows</code>, <code>Cool Water Melody Ranch</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Gene Sings &#39;Cool Water&#39; and &#39;Buttons and Bows&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX / WABC)</strong><br>1948-08-22<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Wm. Wrigley Jr. Co.</strong><br>Doublemint Chewing Gum</td>\n<td><strong>Gene Autry</strong>, <strong>Pat Buttram</strong>, <strong>The Cass County Boys</strong>, <strong>The Pinafores</strong>, <strong>Carl Cotner Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS Hollywood</strong>, <strong>Gene Twombly</strong> (Sound Effects), <strong>Charlie Lyon</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Gene Autry and Pat Buttram welcome listeners to Melody Ranch, with Gene performing &#39;Cool Water&#39; and &#39;Buttons and Bows&#39;, followed by comedic frontier horse-trading tall tales with Pat Buttram and western swing by the Cass County Boys.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gene_Autry_1954_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Pat Buttram&#39;s Uranium Mine</code>, <code>Melody Ranch 1954</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Pat Buttram&#39;s Uranium Claim Scheme&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX)</strong><br>1954-08-22<br>7:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Wm. Wrigley Jr. Co.</strong><br>Doublemint Chewing Gum</td>\n<td><strong>Gene Autry</strong>, <strong>Pat Buttram</strong>, <strong>The Cass County Boys</strong>, <strong>Carl Cotner&#39;s Melody Ranch Band</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Flying A / Wrigley</strong>, <strong>Johnny Jacobs</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Gene Autry sings &#39;Back in the Saddle Again&#39; and &#39;I&#39;m an Old Cowhand&#39;, while Pat Buttram spins a hilarious scheme about staking out a bogus uranium claim in Death Valley with a clicking pocket watch he mistook for a Geiger counter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Quiz_Kids_1943_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Joel Kupperman Math</code>, <code>Quiz Kids Alka-Seltzer 165</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Seven-Year-Old Joel Kupperman Calculates Mental Cube Roots&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue / ABC (WENR)</strong><br>1943-08-22<br>7:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Miles Laboratories</strong><br>Alka-Seltzer &amp; One-A-Day Vitamins</td>\n<td><strong>Joe Kelly</strong> (Quizmaster), <strong>Joel Kupperman</strong> (Age 7), <strong>Gerard Darrow</strong> (Age 10), <strong>Richard Williams</strong> (Age 13), <strong>Harve Fischman</strong> (Age 13)</td>\n<td><strong>Louis G. Cowan</strong> (Creator &amp; Producer), <strong>John Lewellen</strong> (Research), <strong>Bob Murphy</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Quiz&quot;, &quot;Game Show&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Seven-year-old mathematical prodigy Joel Kupperman stuns quizmaster Joe Kelly by performing lightning-fast mental cube root calculations in seconds, alongside 10-year-old naturalist Gerard Darrow identifying rare South American bird calls from listener mail questions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Comic_Weekly_1948_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Puck Comic Weekly</code>, <code>Blondie &amp; Flash Gordon</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Reading the Sunday Comics: Blondie, Flash Gordon, and Popeye&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WNEW New York</strong><br>1948-08-22<br>8:30 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Hearst Newspapers</strong><br>Puck - The Comic Weekly</td>\n<td><strong>Jack Johnstone</strong> (The Comic Weekly Man / Storyteller)</td>\n<td><strong>WNEW Production Staff</strong>, New York</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Children&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The Comic Weekly Man reads Sunday newspaper comic strips over the air with animated character voices and sound effects, bringing to life the latest misadventures of &#39;Blondie and Dagwood&#39;, &#39;Flash Gordon&#39;, &#39;Prince Valiant&#39;, and &#39;Popeye&#39; from Puck The Comic Weekly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Comic_Weekly_1954_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Sunday Funnies 1954</code>, <code>Beetle Bailey Snuffy Smith</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Reading the Sunday Comics: Beetle Bailey, Snuffy Smith, and The Phantom&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WNEW New York</strong><br>1954-08-22<br>8:30 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Hearst Newspapers</strong><br>Puck - The Comic Weekly</td>\n<td><strong>Jack Johnstone</strong> (The Comic Weekly Man / Narrator)</td>\n<td><strong>WNEW Radio</strong>, New York</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Children&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The Comic Weekly Man delights Sunday morning young listeners by dramatically reading aloud and voicing the weekend color comic strips, featuring the comic escapades of &#39;Beetle Bailey&#39; dodging Sergeant Snorkel, &#39;Snuffy Smith&#39; in the hills, and &#39;The Phantom&#39; in the Deep Woods.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 92</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
