    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American network and syndicated broadcasting history (1943–1951):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Mr_DA_1945_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Old-Fashioned Murder</code>, <code>Victorian Arsenic Case</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Old-Fashioned Murder&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1945-08-22<br>9:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Bristol-Myers Company</strong><br>Vitalis Hair Tonic &amp; Sal Hepatica</td>\n<td><strong>Jay Jostyn</strong> (D.A. Paul Garrett), <strong>Vicki Vola</strong> (Miss Miller), <strong>Len Doyle</strong> (Harrington), <strong>Paul Stewart</strong> (Carl Thorne)</td>\n<td><strong>Jerry Devine</strong> (Writer), <strong>Edward A. Byron</strong> (Director), <strong>Jerry O&#39;Brien</strong> (Sound Effects), <strong>Fred Cole</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>District Attorney Paul Garrett investigates the poisoning of a wealthy antique dealer whose family members were methodically murdered using 19th-century Victorian poisons, tracing the lethal concoction to an apothecary ledger kept by the victim&#39;s nephew.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mr_DA_1951_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Change for a Hundred</code>, <code>Counterfeit C-Note</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of Change for a Hundred&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1951-08-22<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Bristol-Myers Company</strong><br>Vitalis &amp; Bufferin (&quot;Twice as fast as aspirin&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Jay Jostyn</strong> (Paul Garrett), <strong>Vicki Vola</strong> (Miss Miller), <strong>Len Doyle</strong> (Harrington), <strong>Ralph Bell</strong> (Marty Finch)</td>\n<td><strong>Jerry Devine</strong> (Writer), <strong>Edward A. Byron</strong> (Director), <strong>Fred Cole</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Paul Garrett and investigator Harrington coordinate with U.S. Secret Service agents to smash a high-stakes counterfeiting operation passing bogus hundred-dollar bills across downtown department stores and racetrack betting windows.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Soldiers_Press_1943_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>John Mecklin - Sicilian Invasion</code>, <code>Landing in Sicily</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;John Mecklin and the Sicilian Invasion&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>United Press</strong><br>1943-08-22<br>(Syndicated Disc No. 49)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>UP Wartime Morale Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>United Press Radio Staff</strong>, Professional Radio Cast dramatizing <strong>John Mecklin</strong></td>\n<td><strong>UP Radio News Division</strong> (Producers), <strong>Hugh James</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Documentary&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>United Press war correspondent John Mecklin lands with Allied amphibious forces on the beaches of Gela during Operation Husky, braving enemy Stuka dive-bombing and artillery fire to file frontline eyewitness dispatches on the capture of Palermo.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Skippy_Theatre_1947_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Song in the Dark</code>, <code>Skippy Theatre 192</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Song in the Dark (Starring Nina Foch)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KNX / MacGregor</strong><br>1947-08-22<br>(Hollywood Transcription)</td>\n<td><strong>Rosefield Packing Co.</strong><br>Skippy Peanut Butter</td>\n<td><strong>Nina Foch</strong> (Martha Campbell), <strong>Gerald Mohr</strong> (David Reynolds), <strong>Les Mitchel</strong> (Host)</td>\n<td><strong>Les Mitchel</strong> (Producer &amp; Director), <strong>Mahlon Merrick</strong> (Music), <strong>Ken Peters</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A blind concert pianist believes she is being stalked by an obsessive composer who whistles a distinctive melody outside her apartment window, enlisting an empathetic doctor to determine whether the stalker is real or a product of trauma.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gabriel_Heatter_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>We&#39;re Halfway Across France</code>, <code>Good News Tonight</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;We&#39;re Halfway Across France: Liberation of Paris Approaches&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>R.B. Semler, Inc.</strong><br>Kreml Hair Tonic &amp; Kreml Shampoo</td>\n<td><strong>Gabriel Heatter</strong> (News Analyst / Commentator)</td>\n<td><strong>Mutual News Division</strong>, <strong>Frank Waldecker</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;News&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Gabriel Heatter delivers his famous opening phrase &#39;There is good news tonight!&#39;, passionately analyzing General Patton&#39;s Third Army sweeping across the Seine River and French Resistance fighters rising up to liberate Paris from Nazi occupation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Spotlight_Bands_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Romance and Music</code>, <code>Jan Garber Dale Mabry</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Jan Garber Remote from Dale Mabry Field, Florida&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue / ABC</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>9:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Coca-Cola Company</strong><br>&quot;Victory Parade of Spotlight Bands&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Jan Garber and his Orchestra</strong> (featuring vocalists <strong>Bob Davis</strong> and <strong>Liz Tilton</strong>)</td>\n<td><strong>The Coca-Cola Company</strong> (Production), <strong>Gil Martyn</strong> (Announcer), Dale Mabry Field FL</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Jan Garber and his Orchestra perform live before thousands of Army Air Force servicemen at Dale Mabry Field in Florida, delivering spirited swing numbers including &#39;My Heart Tells Me&#39;, &#39;Besame Mucho&#39;, and &#39;Jersey Bounce&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 80</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
