    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American and South African broadcasting history (1934–1980):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Camel_Caravan_1935_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Palomar Ballroom Remote</code>, <code>Benny Goodman Palomar</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Benny Goodman Remote from the Palomar Ballroom&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC / KNX)</strong><br>1935-08-22<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>R.J. Reynolds Tobacco Co.</strong><br>Camel Cigarettes &amp; Prince Albert Tobacco</td>\n<td><strong>Benny Goodman and his Orchestra</strong> (featuring Gene Krupa on drums, Bunny Berigan on trumpet, Jess Stacy on piano), <strong>Helen Ward</strong> (Vocalist)</td>\n<td><strong>William Esty Agency</strong> (Producers), <strong>Jack Johnstone</strong> (Announcer), Palomar Ballroom LA</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Benny Goodman and his Orchestra perform their historic Palomar Ballroom engagement in Los Angeles, igniting the Swing Era with electrifying arrangements of &#39;King Porter Stomp&#39;, &#39;Down South Camp Meetin&#39;&#39;, and &#39;Sometimes I&#39;m Happy&#39; featuring drummer Gene Krupa and trumpeter Bunny Berigan.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Frank_Watanabe_1934_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Archie&#39;s New Golf Clubs</code>, <code>Frank Watanabe 112</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Archie&#39;s Antique Golf Clubs&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KNX Los Angeles</strong><br>1934-08-22<br>(Pacific Coast)</td>\n<td><strong>Regional Sponsors</strong><br>Union Oil &amp; Local Dairies</td>\n<td><strong>Eddie Holden</strong> (Frank Watanabe), <strong>Reginald Sharland</strong> (Archie Higginbotham)</td>\n<td><strong>Eddie Holden &amp; Reginald Sharland</strong> (Writers &amp; Producers)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Dialect&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Well-meaning Japanese houseboy Frank Watanabe attempts to polish Archie&#39;s antique Scottish golf clubs with corrosive silver dip, creating comedic panic when Archie discovers his prized clubs warped on the morning of an important club championship match.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stoopnagle_Bud_1934_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Inventions For Useless Occasions</code>, <code>Reversible Doughnut</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Inventions for Useless Occasions&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1934-08-22<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>General Motors</strong><br>Pontiac Motor Division (&quot;Silver Streak&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>F. Chase Taylor</strong> (Col. Lemuel Q. Stoopnagle), <strong>Budd Hulick</strong> (Budd), <strong>Victor Arden Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>F. Chase Taylor</strong> (Writer), <strong>Al Scott</strong> (Sound Effects), <strong>Louis Dean</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Colonel Stoopnagle demonstrates his latest absurd inventions—including a glass flyswatter for seeing what you missed and a square bathtub to prevent ring-around-the-tub—while trading surreal non-sequiturs and comedic barbs with partner Budd Hulick.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Squad_Cars_1969_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The R30,000-00 Question</code>, <code>Rand Extortion Case</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The R30,000-00 Question&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Springbok Radio (SABC)</strong><br>1969-08-22<br>8:00 PM SAST</td>\n<td><strong>Castrol South Africa</strong><br>Castrol GTX Motor Oil</td>\n<td><strong>Colin Fisher</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Graham Armitage</strong> (Inspector van der Merwe), <strong>Douglas Bristow</strong> (Sgt. Pretorius), <strong>Hugh Rouse</strong> (Frank Henderson)</td>\n<td><strong>David Gooden</strong> (Writer &amp; Director), SABC Johannesburg Studios</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Police Procedural&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Flying Squad officers in Johannesburg investigate a ruthless telephone extortionist threatening to detonate a home-made explosive inside a crowded suburban supermarket unless 30,000 Rand is dropped in a designated trash receptacle at the railway station.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Squad_Cars_1975_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Brake Fluid</code>, <code>Sabotage Chapman&#39;s Peak</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Brake Fluid&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Springbok Radio (SABC)</strong><br>1975-08-22<br>8:00 PM SAST</td>\n<td><strong>Datsun-Nissan SA</strong><br>Firestone Tires</td>\n<td><strong>Colin Fisher</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Graham Armitage</strong> (Detective Sergeant), <strong>Anthony Fridjhon</strong> (Constable Ross), <strong>Patrick Mynhardt</strong> (Hans Becker)</td>\n<td><strong>David Gooden</strong> (Writer &amp; Director), SABC Studios</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Police Procedural&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Police accident investigators discover drained hydraulic brake fluid and severed copper tubing on a sports car that plunged off the treacherous Chapman&#39;s Peak Drive, exposing a calculated murder plotted by the victim&#39;s heavily indebted business partner.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Squad_Cars_1980_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Phone Call</code>, <code>Midnight Stalker Durban</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Phone Call&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Springbok Radio (SABC)</strong><br>1980-08-22<br>8:00 PM SAST</td>\n<td><strong>Mobil Oil Southern Africa</strong><br>Toyota South Africa</td>\n<td><strong>Colin Fisher</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Dale Cutts</strong> (Inspector Nel), <strong>Nigel Vermaas</strong> (Sergeant Botha), <strong>Diane Appleby</strong> (Claire Vance)</td>\n<td><strong>David Gooden</strong> (Director), SABC Commercial Service</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Police Procedural&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Flying Squad detectives trace a series of terrifying midnight telephone threats targeting a Durban hospital nurse, uncovering a revenge scheme orchestrated by a recently paroled burglar she testified against three years prior.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 62</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
